 CellChangeTimes->{3.988820413791424*^9},
 CellLabel->
  "Out[178]=",ExpressionUUID->"16011a83-f803-4cc6-a03c-9c8bc7e7b207"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Built-in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{"Symmetrize", "paclet:ref/Symmetrize"},
   "RefLink"]], "Text",
  FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->
  "3b5543d2-661d-4659-8a98-b1a6e34874a2"],
 " produces a tensor with the three pair symmetries, but the Bianchi sum has ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"O", "(", "1", ")"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  ExpressionUUID->"c5a95f3b-68b0-44ea-8997-71587c64eec7"],
 " residual. No mono-term language can fix this: Bianchi asks three permuted \
copies to sum to zero, which is two relations short of the single-permutation \
form ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{"Symmetrize", "paclet:ref/Symmetrize"},
   "RefLink"]], "Text",
  FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->
  "987f44db-79ec-46cf-8874-733cd96e3ff6"],
 " understands. The corresponding count is visible in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
   "SymmetrizedIndependentComponents", 
    "paclet:ref/SymmetrizedIndependentComponents"},
   "RefLink"]], "Text",
  FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->
  "ff5f808f-7035-40cd-b2eb-2028b1522405"],
 ": at dimension 4 with the three mono-term Riemann generators the kernel \
reports 21 independent components, whereas the true number of Riemann \
components (post-Bianchi) is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["n", "2"], "(", 
    SuperscriptBox["n", "2"], "-", "1", ")", "/", "12", "=", "20"}], 
   TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "beeaa90e-e96c-49b9-acae-44b74195d6cd"],
 ". The single missing relation is Bianchi."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"f632b2eb-33d6-406e-b553-16d9a77e173c"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
The Young-projector solution: discovering the algebraic identities\
\>", "Subsection",ExpressionUUID->"608c3350-ecc0-4788-bc7f-34c7ce921502"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "*The four classical Riemann symmetries are not separately ",
 StyleBox["assumed",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " but ",
 StyleBox["derived",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " from the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"{", " ", "2", ",", "2", " ", "}"}], TraditionalForm]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"3e40a357-fd1b-4812-80bb-f952db5bb2b6"],
 " projector itself. Searching the right-annihilator of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["R", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "2bf5e984-3ee5-4a3c-86e0-6f24e054875c"],
 " in the group algebra ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"\[DoubleStruckCapitalC]", "[", 
    SubscriptBox["S", "4"], "]"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  ExpressionUUID->"f7627275-3749-47ab-878c-5ea6311d653b"],
 " recovers the textbook first Bianchi identity as one specific kernel \
element, without naming it in advance.*"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"c19b7608-d41c-49bf-a95f-90b9e822ec44"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"{", " ", "2", ",", "2", " ", "}"}], TraditionalForm]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"f42713ef-cde9-44ea-a257-ef8533136a6a"],
 " Young projector ",
 StyleBox["defines",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " the Riemann irreducible representation: every tensor in its image \
satisfies the four classical Riemann identities by construction. The more \
interesting move is the reverse direction, ",
 StyleBox["deriving those identities from the projector",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", without listing them in advance. The Symmetry functions make this \
constructive rather than verifying."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"9a70afba-a6d3-4840-9c70-073491b57e3a"],

Cell["\<\
Begin by fixing a slot labelling: build the rank-4 Young tableau whose two \
columns hold the two antisymmetric pairs and whose two rows hold the \
pair-swap-symmetric slots.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"b107adca-23d0-4bdb-afb7-11730d164fcc"],

